

{% assign product = closest.product %}

{% if product != blank and product.available and settings.quick_add %}
  <div class="lambruk-add-button">
    {% render 'quick-add', product: product, section_id: section.id %}
  </div>
{% endif %}

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
    Repositions Horizon's native quick-add from a hover-revealed circular
    overlay on the card image into a persistent, always-visible button in
    the price row. The quick-add snippet itself (variant resolution,
    availability, the Add/Choose branch, cart submission) is untouched —
    only its container's positioning and the button's own visual styling
    are overridden here. See ARCHITECTURE.md's upgrade log for the design
    spec this was measured against.
  */
  .lambruk-add-button {
    display: inline-flex;
  }

  .lambruk-add-button .quick-add {
    display: inline-flex;
    position: static;
    inset: unset;
    pointer-events: auto;
    z-index: auto;
  }

  .lambruk-add-button .quick-add__button {
    display: flex !important;
    opacity: 1 !important;
    position: static;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--gap-2xs);
    width: auto;
    max-inline-size: none;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    height: 36px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid #A07037;
    background: transparent;
    color: #131A3E;
    font-family: var(--font-body--family);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    text-transform: none;
    overflow: visible;
    backdrop-filter: none;
    transition-property: background-color, border-color;
  }

  .lambruk-add-button .add-to-cart-icon {
    display: none;
  }

  .lambruk-add-button .quick-add__button:is(:hover, :focus-visible) {
    width: auto;
    padding-inline-end: 16px;
    background: #EFE9E1;
    border-color: #A07037;
  }

  /*
    Sale price: wrap, not hide/truncate. A sale price (current + struck-
    through compare-at, both rendered by snippets/price.liquid as sibling
    spans inside one [ref="priceContainer"]) plus the button don't fit on
    one line at a 2-up mobile card's ~123px price-row width. Chosen over
    hiding the compare-at price (the first fix shipped here): hiding
    removes the only discount signal for the majority-mobile audience it
    matters most for, and a live test confirmed the layout risk that would
    argue for hiding doesn't actually hold — CSS Grid's default
    align-items: stretch keeps every card in a shared grid row aligned
    even when one wraps taller. Confirmed by forcing one card into the
    wrapped state and measuring it against its untouched neighbour: both
    grew from 286.19px to 320.59px together, bottoms stayed exactly
    aligned. The cost is a non-sale neighbour gaining dead space to match
    the taller card — the same row-height dynamic this grid already has
    from variable title-wrap lengths, not a new failure mode.

    :has(.compare-at-price) scopes this to rows that actually have a sale
    price — .compare-at-price only exists in the DOM when
    show_compare_price is true in snippets/price.liquid, so every
    single-price card (all 52 products in the catalogue as of 2026-08-30)
    never matches this selector, at any viewport, and is untouched by it.

    No real sale product exists in the catalog to verify this against —
    simulated via injected markup matching snippets/price.liquid's actual
    sale-price output exactly.

    !important on flex-wrap: this file's own selector and the pre-existing
    `.product-card .group-block-content.layout-panel-flex--row { flex-wrap:
    nowrap; }` rule (in this block's own stylesheet, written earlier for a
    different row — the homepage's title+price row) compute to the exact
    same specificity, (0,3,0). Confirmed live: without !important, nowrap
    silently won and the wrap never engaged. Not a hack — matches this
    project's own established precedent for the same situation (see the
    --gallery-aspect-ratio fix, ARCHITECTURE.md §43).

    justify-content override on .shopify-product-form: quick-add's own
    markup nests the actual button inside two further custom elements
    (<product-form-component>, then a real <form class="shopify-product-form">)
    that were never designed to be measured for their own content width —
    they were always rendered inside the original absolute-positioned image
    overlay, which supplied their sizing context for free via inset/
    max-inline-size. Once wrapped onto its own line, <product-form-component>
    resolves to 0 width (confirmed live, not assumed — traced through every
    nested element via getBoundingClientRect), and quick-add-styles.liquid's
    own `justify-content: flex-end` on .shopify-product-form then anchors the
    button's RIGHT edge to that zero-width point, so the button renders
    58.375px to the LEFT of where it should be — confirmed live at
    left: -29.375px, visibly detached from the row. Forcing explicit pixel
    widths through every nested layer (product-form-component, quick-add
    itself) was tried first and kept breaking a different layer each time
    it fixed the one above — not a stable fix. Overriding just this one
    property is: flex-start packs the button against its container's START
    edge instead, which resolves correctly even against a zero-width
    container. Scoped to .lambruk-add-button only, so the real product-page
    add-to-cart form (a different .shopify-product-form entirely, in a
    totally different part of the DOM) is untouched.
  */
  .group-block-content:has(> .lambruk-add-button):has(.compare-at-price) {
    flex-wrap: wrap !important;
  }

  .group-block-content:has(> .lambruk-add-button):has(.compare-at-price)
    product-price {
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .group-block-content:has(> .lambruk-add-button):has(.compare-at-price)
    .lambruk-add-button
    .shopify-product-form {
    justify-content: flex-start;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  /*
    Suppresses Horizon's native hover-pill quick-add (still rendered by the
    unmodified _product-card-gallery block on the card's image) wherever
    this button is present. :has() scopes this to only the four templates
    that include _lambruk-add-button — the homepage Top Sellers rail and
    product-page recommendations never render this block, so their native
    hover pill is untouched. :has() is already used extensively elsewhere
    in Horizon (assets/base.css, etc.), confirmed safe for this project's
    browser support baseline.
  */
  product-card:has(.lambruk-add-button) .card-gallery .quick-add {
    display: none !important;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Add button (price row)",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
